<div class="page-title">
  <h1>Under the hood</h1>
  <p class="lead">The detail for anyone who wants to check our work: roles, the full message flow, the safety rules and what we haven't tested yet.</p>
</div>
<article class="notebook">
  <section class="notebook-entry" aria-labelledby="roles">
    <aside class="entry-margin">
      <p class="margin-note">People in the room</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="entry-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="entry-content">
      <p class="entry-kicker">01 · ROLES</p>
      <h2 id="roles">Roles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The human owns the project access, posts the request, and approves one labelled option in chat.</li>
        <li>The maintainer agent reads the selected repo through a read-only GitHub connection, groups results by environment, and proposes options when a test fails.</li>
        <li>Each volunteer runs the quickstart (the steps a new user follows to install your project and get it running) on their own machine and reports the command and result.</li>
        <li>Switch holds the room, applies message-addressing rules, and delivers targeted messages between agents.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="notebook-entry" aria-labelledby="flow">
    <aside class="entry-margin">
      <p class="margin-note">The order</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="entry-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="entry-content">
      <p class="entry-kicker">02 · FLOW</p>
      <h2 id="flow">Flow</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>The maintainer command creates a room and maintainer agent from a template. The setup script places the maintainer's OpenCode config in its workdir before any request is posted.</li>
        <li>Each volunteer joins from their own machine. The script places the volunteer config and clones this public repo before a session starts.</li>
        <li>The human pastes the printed request into the kickoff thread and @mentions the maintainer.</li>
        <li>Volunteers follow the numbered steps, then send each requested result to the maintainer with a targeted message and post it in the kickoff thread.</li>
        <li>Before waiting for a volunteer, the maintainer reads the thread context once in case the result is already there. It groups the results by environment and reads only the source files needed to explain a failure.</li>
        <li>If everyone passes, the maintainer posts a short all-pass report and doesn't ask for approval. If a step fails, it cites the source and offers labelled options with their tradeoffs and exact changes.</li>
        <li>The human approves one option in chat. The maintainer sends only that local change to the volunteers who need to rerun. It posts a final report in the thread. No agent changes the project repo.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="notebook-entry" aria-labelledby="safety-model">
    <aside class="entry-margin">
      <p class="margin-note">What can run</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="entry-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="entry-content">
      <p class="entry-kicker">03 · SAFETY MODEL</p>
      <h2 id="safety-model">Safety model</h2>
      <p>Volunteers use the root opencode.json from this repo. Bash is denied by default. These are the allowed patterns:</p>
      <div class="spec-block" aria-label="Volunteer Bash allowlist">
        <pre class="command-block"><code>mkdir *
cd *
python3 *
cat *
ls
pwd
python3 --version
git clone *</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p>OpenCode edits stay inside the volunteer workdir. Access to external directories and web fetching are denied. The setup script clones the public repo into that workdir before any session starts.</p>
      <p>Cross-owner messages are blocked by default. A volunteer keeps its owner rule and gets one room-scoped exception for the maintainer agent and its linked messaging identity. In Solo, both agents share an owner and use owner-agents addressing.</p>
      <p>The maintainer config denies OpenCode tools by default, then allows Switch tools and a narrow Bash rule for <code>gh api repos/*</code> with write flags denied. It reads project files only with GET requests like this:</p>
      <pre class="command-block"><code>gh api repos/{project_repo}/contents/&lt;path&gt; -H "Accept: application/vnd.github.raw"</code></pre>
      <p>The maintainer numbers the returned lines and cites full repo-relative paths. It doesn't clone or download source files, run other commands, start subagents, or write to GitHub or Google. Its workdir contains only opencode.json.</p>
      <p>The human must approve a labelled option before the maintainer sends a fix. The fix stays in a volunteer workdir. The human applies any project change separately.</p>
      <p>These guardrails aren't a sandbox for hostile code. Every allowed Python command can run arbitrary Python code. Review the quickstart before inviting volunteers and use a fresh workdir.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

